% ============================================================================
% conjunction-type — three named categories of conjunction and what each
% actually does (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A conjunction's JOB depends on WHICH kind it is -- some conjunctions join
% equal-rank pieces of a sentence, some come in matched pairs, and some
% attach a dependent clause onto an independent one. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? conjunction_type(subordinating_conjunction, $D)
%       % joins_dependent_clauses_to_independent_clauses
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `conjunction_type(type, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a conjunction-related term that is not one
% of these three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and
% recall the type it names:
%
%     ? conjunction_type($T, are_pairs_of_conjunctions_that_work_together)
%       % correlative_conjunction
%
% The three conjunction types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                          description                                                     quote (verbatim)
%     -----------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     coordinating_conjunction       joins_words_phrases_and_clauses_of_equal_grammatical_rank         "Coordinating conjunctions allow you to join words, phrases, and clauses of equal grammatical rank in a sentence."
%     correlative_conjunction        are_pairs_of_conjunctions_that_work_together                      "Correlative conjunctions are pairs of conjunctions that work together by framing and connecting grammatically identical sentence elements."
%     subordinating_conjunction      joins_dependent_clauses_to_independent_clauses                    "Subordinating conjunctions join dependent clauses to the independent clauses of sentences, signaling cause and effect, comparison, contrast, time, or some other kind of relationship between the clauses."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Conjunctions" article,
% which introduces each of these three categories in its own complete
% sentence before moving on to worked examples. WebFetch-verified before
% writing (twice — the second pass specifically re-confirmed the
% coordinating and correlative sentences were complete, with no truncation
% or missing clauses). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable
% secondary reference, the same tier the sibling `noun-type.adj`/
% `verb-type.adj`/`pronoun-type.adj`/`preposition-type.adj` (the other
% libraries in this directory) already use for their Grammarly citations.
% A conjunction-related term that is NOT one of these three — "conjunctive_adverb",
% a real category the same page also names ("A conjunctive adverb is an
% adverb that does the job of a conjunction"), but one that belongs to a
% DIFFERENT word class (an adverb, not a conjunction) rather than being a
% fourth type of conjunction itself — is deliberately NOT a row, so a
% recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a description. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table conjunction_type {
    columns type, description

    row (coordinating_conjunction, joins_words_phrases_and_clauses_of_equal_grammatical_rank)
    row (correlative_conjunction, are_pairs_of_conjunctions_that_work_together)
    row (subordinating_conjunction, joins_dependent_clauses_to_independent_clauses)

    source "Coordinating conjunctions allow you to join words, phrases, and clauses of equal grammatical rank in a sentence."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/conjunctions/"
    trust consensus
}
